# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI coding agents working in the **{{ project_name }}** repository, which was
generated from the [NOVA application template]({{ _src_path }}) using
[copier](https://copier.readthedocs.io/).
{% if application_type == 'Minimal' %}
This is the Minimal flavor: there is no Python package here, only deployment configuration and
shell scripts.

## Layout

```text
minimal_config.yaml    project name and version (read by CI and by the deploy script)
xml/tool.xml           Galaxy tool definition (placeholders filled in at deploy time)
scripts/init-repo.sh   initializes the git repository and remote
scripts/deploy-tool.sh pushes the tool XML to the Galaxy tools repository
dockerfiles/Dockerfile container image used by CI and by Galaxy
.gitlab-ci.yml         build and publish pipelines
```

The toolchain is bash, docker, git, sed and grep — there is no Python environment to activate.

## Versioning and deployment

- The `version` field in `minimal_config.yaml` is the single source of truth. CI and
  `scripts/deploy-tool.sh` both read it; bump it there, nowhere else.
- `xml/tool.xml` is a template: the deploy script substitutes `@TOOL_VERSION@`,
  `@CONTAINER_REGISTRY@`, `@CONTAINER_PATH@` and `@CONTAINER_TAG@`. Leave those placeholders in
  place and never commit resolved values.
- Build and push the Docker image before deploying the tool XML:
  `docker build -f dockerfiles/Dockerfile -t {{ project_name.lower().replace(' ', '-').replace('_', '-') }} .`
  The image must still be able to run as a non-root user.
- `scripts/init-repo.sh` and `scripts/deploy-tool.sh` push to shared remotes (the project's GitLab
  repository and the Galaxy tools repository). **Do not run them on your own initiative** — ask
  the user to run them, or to confirm first.

## Shell script conventions

- Keep `set -e` at the top of scripts, quote variable expansions, and keep the confirmation
  prompts in the deploy scripts — they are the only guard against an accidental push.
- Stick to the tools the scripts already assume (`bash`, `git`, `docker`, `sed`, `grep`,
  `mktemp`); do not introduce new runtime dependencies.

